      {
        "id": "p27-q65",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$2^{2y}+2^y-x=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p27-q65-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=\\log_2\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{1+4x}}{2}\\right),\\qquad x>0",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p27-q65-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Solve the quadratic in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "2^y",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and retain its positive root.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $u=2^y>0$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}\nu^2+u-x&=0\\\\\nu&=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{1+4x}}{2}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe minus root is negative. The plus root is positive exactly when $x>0$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}\n2^y&=\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{1+4x}}{2}\\\\\ny&=\\log_2\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{1+4x}}{2}\\right),\\qquad x>0.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThere is no real value of $y$ for $x\\le0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 62
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q66",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$9^y-3^{y+1}-1+x=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p27-q66-short-plus",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=\\log_3\\left(\\frac{3+\\sqrt{13-4x}}{2}\\right),\\qquad x\\le\\frac{13}{4}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p27-q66-short-minus",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Also ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=\\log_3\\left(\\frac{3-\\sqrt{13-4x}}{2}\\right),\\qquad 1<x\\le\\frac{13}{4}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ". The branches coincide at ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x=\\frac{13}{4}",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p27-q66-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Solve the quadratic in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "3^y",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and retain only positive real roots.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $u=3^y>0$. Since $9^y=u^2$ and $3^{y+1}=3u$,\n$$\\begin{align*}\nu^2-3u+x-1&=0\\\\\nu&=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt{9-4(x-1)}}{2}\\\\\nu&=\\frac{3\\pm\\sqrt{13-4x}}{2}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nReal roots require $x\\le\\frac{13}{4}$. The plus root is positive throughout this range, giving\n$$y=\\log_3\\left(\\frac{3+\\sqrt{13-4x}}{2}\\right),\\qquad x\\le\\frac{13}{4}.$$\nThe minus root is positive only when $\\sqrt{13-4x}<3$, that is, when $x>1$. It therefore gives\n$$y=\\log_3\\left(\\frac{3-\\sqrt{13-4x}}{2}\\right),\\qquad 1<x\\le\\frac{13}{4}.$$\nAt $x=\\frac{13}{4}$, both branches give $y=\\log_3\\left(\\frac32\\right)$. For $x>\\frac{13}{4}$ there is no real solution.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 108
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q67",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$e^{2y}+e^y-1=x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p27-q67-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "y=\\ln\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{4x+5}}{2}\\right),\\qquad x>-1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p27-q67-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Solve the quadratic in ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "e^y",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " and retain its positive root.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Let $u=e^y>0$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}\nu^2+u-(x+1)&=0\\\\\nu&=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{1+4(x+1)}}{2}\\\\\nu&=\\frac{-1\\pm\\sqrt{4x+5}}{2}.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe minus root is negative. The plus root is positive exactly when $x>-1$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}\ne^y&=\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{4x+5}}{2}\\\\\ny&=\\ln\\left(\\frac{-1+\\sqrt{4x+5}}{2}\\right),\\qquad x>-1.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThere is no real value of $y$ for $x\\le-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 72
      }
